  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its norm and Borel measurable structure; dimension zero is permitted. Let V:E->R be globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable, let alpha and beta be nonnegative real numbers, and let eta be real. Assume 0<alpha<=beta, 0<eta, beta*eta<=1, and, for every x,a in E,\nalpha*||a||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[a,a] <= beta*||a||^2.\nDefine mu as canonical volume tilted by -V, using exponential weight exp(-V) divided by its genuine Bochner normalizer. Define J as the pushforward of mu times the centered identity-covariance Gaussian on E under (x,g)->(x,x+sqrt(eta)*g), and let nu be J's second marginal.\n\nThen mu, J, and nu are probability measures. There exist two measurable Markov kernels R,S:E->Measures(E), with probability fibers at every point, such that R is a conditional kernel of the swapped joint measure: nu.compProd(R)=J.map(swap). For every y in E, S(y) is the pushforward of R(y) under x->2*x-y. Moreover, for every y in E, S(y) is canonical volume tilted by the function\nu -> -V((y+u)/2) - ||y-u||^2/(8*eta),\nwith its genuine exponential normalizer.\n\nThese same kernels R and S work for every globally smooth, compactly supported real function f on E. For each such f, define the actual integral Tf(y)=integral f(u) dS(y)(u) at every y. The function Tf is globally Frechet differentiable, and f, Tf, and the genuine gradient of Tf all belong to L^2(nu), meaning almost-everywhere strong measurability and finite L^2 seminorm. The function y->Var_{S(y)}(f) is integrable with respect to nu, where\nVar_{S(y)}(f)=integral (f(u)-integral f(v) dS(y)(v))^2 dS(y)(u).\nThe following exact identity and inequality hold:\nintegral Var_{S(y)}(f) dnu(y)\n = integral f(y)^2 dnu(y) - integral Tf(y)^2 dnu(y),\neta * integral ||gradient Tf(y)||^2 dnu(y)\n <= ((1-alpha*eta)^2/(4*(1+alpha*eta)))\n    * (integral f(y)^2 dnu(y) - integral Tf(y)^2 dnu(y)).\nThe gradient is obtained by inverse Riesz from the genuine real Frechet derivative of Tf. All the measures, kernels, domains, differentiability, L^2 memberships, variance integrability, the identity, and the inequality are outputs under the stated analytic assumptions; no such certificate is supplied as an additional premise.",
